Z||000022||JIM GOOSE||03-16-2000||11:20 AM||||If you do business with a credit card,youll be able to dispute charges and usually be satisfied. Cash,check,money order and you are taking a chance on losing your money for a lot of reasons. Its obvious that you should get product or money back in any event. Sometimes you just have to chalk it up to experience.||||reg||1
Z||000023||william_thorniii||03-16-2000||06:04 PM||||Jim Goose,<P>Good point about credit cards.  Unfortunately when dealing with Fiero parts I find myself dealing with individuals... not companies.  Therefore cash is usually required.  When dealing with any payment sent via. U.S. mail service you do not have to chalk it up to experiences.  The post office covers this under the mail fraud law.  Therefore your course of actions are as simple to go to the website (http://www.usps.gov/websites/depart/inspect/) or down to your local post office and fill out a simple form.  I was forced to follow this course recently and therefore know from experienced.  When I cc'd the vendor the form to the Postmaster, she quickly contacted me and sent my product (although 6 months had passed prior to that point where she refused to answer my correspondance).  <P>Please note, I am not instructing anyone to follow these actions but merely informing you of your rights.  I would strongly suggest following all other means of dispute resolution before brining in the Postmaster.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||10
